  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 116 PM MST Mon Nov 9 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nInitially zonal flow aloft trends more southwesterly through the \nremainder of the day and into tonight as another wave moves onshore \nfrom the Pacific Northwest. One initial impact from this system will \nbe for increased winds and gusts along the immediate eastern slopes \nof the Rocky Mountain Front, though ridgetop stability is rather \nmediocre at this point in the event. Cross barrier flow is largely \naround or less than 50kts at 700mb, so confidence in any strong \nwinds (sustained wind 50+ mph or a gust of 75+ mph) materializing at \nthe surface in the aforementioned area remains low. The other \ninitial impact will be for mainly light mountain precipitation along \nthe Continental Divide tonight into early Tuesday. Snow levels are \nrather high for this event, briefly dropping below 6,500 ft in a few \nlocalized areas. Given precipitation amounts look light and that \nsnow levels are so high, impacts from any snow that falls will be \nminimal. \n\nHeading into the daytime hours Tuesday the flow aloft trends a bit \nmore northwesterly as the main trough axis exits eastward. Daytime \nmixing will result in gusty winds spreading over the remainder of \nthe plains during the day. winds aloft at the top of the mixed layer \ndoes look to briefly flirt with 50kts, but is not forecast to be \npersistent in time nor location. While a few isolated gusts to 58 \nmph will be reasonable tomorrow over the plains, they look too \ninfrequent in any one area to warrant a High Wind Warning. Trends \nwill continue to be monitored for any changes in thinking for this \ntimeframe. \n\nThe latter half of the week will feature a warming trend thanks to \nan upper level ridge building in across the west. Well above average \ntemperatures are forecast, with a few locations approaching daily\nrecord highs at times. The ridge trends eastward ahead of troughing\nthis weekend, though there remains uncertainty how quickly this \ntrend ensues. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWinds Tuesday: The probability for a 58 mph gust near Cut Bank and \nGeyser is roughly 30%. Further west along the immediate eastern \nslopes of the Rocky Mountain Front the probability for a 75 mph gust \nin and around Browning peaks at 20% or so. While a few isolated \ngusts of criteria will occur, they look to be too isolated to \nwarrant High Wind Warnings at this time. -AM",
